          <h2>Executive Summary</h2>
          <ul class="exec-list">
            <li>ASML is the sole global supplier of extreme ultraviolet (EUV) lithography machines, a position built over roughly two decades of R&D that competitors have not been able to replicate at any comparable cost or timeline.</li>
            <li>Every leading-edge chipmaker — TSMC, Samsung, Intel — depends on ASML's roadmap to advance their own process nodes, which gives ASML a level of pricing power and customer lock-in unusual even among semiconductor equipment names.</li>
            <li>The High-NA EUV generation extends this position further out, requiring customers to commit capital years ahead of volume production, which effectively pre-sells ASML's roadmap before revenue is recognised.</li>
            <li>The single largest identifiable risk isn't competitive — it's geopolitical: Dutch and US export restrictions on advanced lithography tools to China have already removed a segment of ASML's addressable market and could tighten further.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>Investment Thesis</h2>
          <p>ASML sits at a genuine chokepoint: no leading-edge logic or memory chip can be made without EUV lithography, and no company besides ASML can supply it. That combination — a single point of failure for an entire industry, held by one company — is rare, and it means ASML's economics are less a bet on which chipmaker wins the AI or smartphone cycle than a bet on the overall trajectory of semiconductor demand, since ASML earns a toll on all of it. The risk to underwrite isn't competition; it's demand cyclicality and export-control policy.</p>

          <h2>Research</h2>

          <h3>1. The EUV Monopoly</h3>
          <p>Extreme ultraviolet lithography — using a 13.5-nanometre wavelength of light to pattern the smallest transistor features — required ASML, Zeiss (optics) and a network of specialist suppliers roughly two decades and tens of billions of dollars of combined investment to commercialise. No competitor has replicated this, and the barrier isn't only capital: it's accumulated engineering knowledge across an entire supply chain that would need to be rebuilt from scratch, at a moment when ASML is already several product generations ahead.</p>

          <h3>2. Customer Lock-In at the Roadmap Level</h3>
          <p>TSMC, Samsung and Intel don't just buy ASML's machines — their own multi-year process roadmaps are built around ASML's tool availability and specifications. That reverses the usual buyer-supplier power dynamic: chipmakers compete with each other for allocation of ASML's most advanced tools, rather than ASML competing for their business. This is the structural reason ASML has historically been able to maintain strong gross margins through a genuinely cyclical end market.</p>

          <h3>3. High-NA EUV — Extending the Runway</h3>
          <p>The next generation of EUV tools, using a higher numerical aperture optical system, pushes resolution further and is being adopted by leading customers to prepare for process nodes still several years from volume production. Because these tools are enormously expensive and take years to install and qualify, customers effectively have to commit capital and lock in orders well ahead of the revenue those nodes will eventually generate — extending ASML's visibility further into the future than is typical for a capital equipment supplier.</p>

          <h3>4. The Risk That Actually Matters</h3>
          <p>Export controls, not competition, are the swing factor. The Dutch government, under pressure from the United States, has restricted sales of ASML's most advanced immersion and EUV tools to China, removing a segment of ASML's historical customer base and introducing genuine policy risk to the growth trajectory — a risk that has nothing to do with ASML's technology and everything to do with the geopolitics of semiconductor supply chains.</p>

